 "steps": [
  {
   "id": "west-deploy",
   "title": "The Western Army takes the hills",
   "time": "Night of 20 to 21 October",
   "narrative": "After losing Gifu Castle and failing to hold Ogaki as a choke point, Ishida Mitsunari marched his coalition west to the basin of Sekigahara. He wanted to fight with the surrounding hills at his back. Ishida stood on the north-west slope, Konishi and Shimazu and Ukita lined the centre, and Otani Yoshitsugu held the south-west end of the line. Kobayakawa Hideaki sat above them all on Mount Matsuo, with the Mori contingent of Kikkawa Hiroie far to the south-east on Mount Nangu. On paper the Western Army was strong. In practice Kobayakawa had been in contact with Kuroda Nagamasa, and Kikkawa had secretly promised that the Mori would not fight.",
